A||||9||Seanh||Oops...Not a good thing.||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||11-20-2000||10:17 PM||||A friend and I were working on my 86 last night. I have the upper plenum off and was working to take the fuel rail assey off. He had already take the spark plugs out and neglected to use a proper spark plug socket, no rubber protecter in there. Anyway, he broke one of the plugs, and upon pulling it away from the engine, a broken peice dropped down the intake manifold and into the head. I remove the middle and lower manifolds to see if I could get it out, and no. I could see it in the head, right on top of the valve. I tried using those long grabber type things (ya know) and could for the life of get a hold of it. I then decided stick-tac on the end of a rod would work, but came up empty handed. I then again tried the grabber things and got somthing out of there, unfortunatly, it was not the ceramic spark plug peice I was looking for. Not, with a peice of wire and a light, I am digging around the valve and I cant see the spark plug peice. What the heck should I do? I just got done replacing the head gaskets and all, and I am definatly not wanting to do it again. I know I probally should go to the trouble of removing the heads and getting it, but do I really have to. Would it cause a huge problem if I didnt? I would think it would probally just fall in the chamber, and be pushed out the exhaust port after an explosion? One thing I leaned is not to let other people work on my car, even though they are just trying to be nice and help. Any ideas on what I should do? I really dont want to take the heads off again before I even get to drive the car again.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-20-2000||11:14 PM||||I suppose you haven't already tried sucking it out of the intake port using a tube duct- taped onto the end of a shop vac's hose? <P>Was that intake valve open (the one that piece of plug fell into)?  If you already installed the rockers, it's a possibility. <BR>And if the valve was open, the piece could even have fallen into the cylinder.  Which means you could do the vacuum tube trick through the spark plug hole, I guess.<P>If no luck, then it's "off with its head!"  BTW, just to tell you what you already knew, next time cover those ports with masking tape  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||9
Z||000002||CraZyRabBit||11-20-2000||11:24 PM||||You might want to try using compressed air. If the valve is closed, cover every other intake port then insert a long tubed nozzle and blow it out. If the valve is opened, blow it out from the spark plug hole, preferably with a engine compression attachment connected to you air compressor. You could make one using a gutted out spark plug welded to an air attachement, understand?<P>Mike. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||overlord||11-20-2000||11:52 PM||||im not sure what spark plugs are made of, but what about a magnet.....i have this long skinny retractable magenet that i have ;used many times picking up nuts and stuff that couldnt get otherwise.||||reg||1
Z||000004||olympic||11-21-2000||12:49 AM||||  I once dropped a piece of plastic into an intake port which I couldn't fish out with anything.  So I removed the spark plug and held a STRONG shop vac on the intake port while I pushed down on the intake valve.  The airflow sucked the piece right out.  Something heavier like porcelin(sp?) might not want to come out though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bill 88||11-21-2000||10:24 AM||||You do not have to thake the head off.  Just close it all up.  Fire it up and go.  Only thing is,in the next 50 to 500 miles, you will probaly have to replace the bent valves, hole in the piston, repair the scratch or gouge on the cylinder wall, maybe the bent push rods and the gaskets to do all this work and the time you will be with out your car and maybe looking for a new(different engine).  I think pulling the head and double checking every thing is actually more cost and time productive than the alternative.  There might be one other piece that you did not see,  there already was one other piece.  Just my .02||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||11-21-2000||12:11 PM||||Sean, You didn't say how big the piece was.<BR>Having seen a 350 that ingested a screw that came out of the bottom of a Quadrathreat carb, I am tempted to say "off with his head". (The engine's. Not the helper's.)<BR>I don't know what porcelain will do if it gets in the way of some moving part. Might crumble. Might not. Worst thing would probably be if it got caught between the valve and seat and held the valve open, in the way of the piston.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <BR>If you can't make absolutely sure the piece is out, another gasket set and a few more hours are way cheaper than scrapping an engine. <BR>Just my $.02.<P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000007||DavidHenshaw||11-21-2000||04:46 PM||||Just my .02 worth. Pull the head and quit thinking about it. You can't risk screwing up the entire engine just to save the trouble of pulling the head and buying another head gasket.<P>Dave<P>86 GT ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||11-21-2000||06:36 PM||||It was a fairly small peice. I would have to say, about the size of a 1/4 of a sunflower seed shell. I am going to be putting in the new 3.4 and taking the car out of commision in January. I would tend to think that it would crumble if it got stuck. At this point I could replace the head gaskets with a blind fold. I am going to just take the damn heads off and clean it out, again. Thanks for the help. I did try a shop vac with no luck. The valve is just begining to be pushed in the open position on the cam, but not far enough to let it fall through. Oh well, Thanks again<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||11-21-2000||08:23 PM||||I don't know why, but it seems those kinds of things used to happen to me <B>all</B> the time when I was younger (fumbling things into carburetors, etc.), and impatient.  Now, they hardly ever do.  Older and wiser? Who knows?  Well, okay, I <B>know</B> I'm older  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!<P>Once I was putting rod bearings into my (turbocharged) Corvair engine, and dropped one of the nuts into the crankcase.  It fell <B>directly</B> under the camshaft!  Great.  My hand wouldn't fit down there. Plus, I didn't want to turn the motor, for fear the nut might scratch the cam.  Finally, my Dad used a long, bent coat hanger with a hook on the end, and deftly retrieved the nut without damaging anything  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!     <P>He also taught me how to use a breaker bar along with a spring scale to torque the nuts (a torque wrench wouldn't fit in there, so had  to improvise).   <P>Anyway, Seanh, I sympathize, and I admire your patience.  As always, good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!||||reg||6
